  "content": {
    "id": "p14-q10-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": null,
    "prompt": "Show that $x+2$ is a factor of\n$$P(x)=2x^3+3x^2-5x-6,$$\nand hence factorise the expression completely.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$P(-2)=0$.\n\n$P(x)=(x+2)(x+1)(2x-3)$.",
      "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}P(-2)&=2(-2)^3+3(-2)^2-5(-2)-6\\\\P(-2)&=-16+12+10-6\\\\P(-2)&=0.\\end{align*}$$\nTherefore, $x+2$ is a factor. By long division,\n$$\\begin{array}{r|l}&2x^2-x-3\\\\\\hline x+2&2x^3+3x^2-5x-6\\\\&-(2x^3+4x^2)\\\\\\hline &-x^2-5x-6\\\\&-(-x^2-2x)\\\\\\hline &-3x-6\\\\&-(-3x-6)\\\\\\hline &0\\end{array}$$\n$$\\begin{align*}P(x)&=(x+2)(2x^2-x-3)\\\\P(x)&=(x+2)(x+1)(2x-3).\\end{align*}$$",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 116
  },
